review_text:
The food here was good, the service was polite and professional, and the atmosphere and ambience were nice. 

While the food quality was good, the portions were disappointingly small. We requested rice, and this may be the only Korean restaurant I've ever been to where the rice bowl wasn't full, but only half full. Our party ordered a $29 order of samgyupsal (pork belly) and were very disappointed to find that the order consisted of three (!) slices, of which a notable portion was just fat. To be honest, I felt cheated when I saw the plate brought out. 

While the servers/waitstaff were polite and nice, the lack of attention--given and altogether available--greatly diminished our experience. Because servers are often standing tableside to cook customers' orders, they are too preoccupied to attend to other tables. Their effort was visible and appreciated. 

I am not sure if this is a common problem here, or if the restaurant was just understaffed when we went. Regardless, I want to emphasize: the service was polite and attentive, but the problem I am describing is a managerial/logistical one. For the scale and size of the operation, there needed to have been a few more bodies on the floor bussing tables, taking orders, etc. We waited an unusually long time between being seated and ordering, as well as between asking for a check and receiving one, but only because all the other servers were busy or running food. 

All this being said, I'd like to end on a good note: 

--Respectable food/ingredient quality;
--Creative, welcome twists on traditional Korean dishes (e.g. was that cinnamon in the soondae? I personally loved it);
--Short wait time (when we went);
--Polite and friendly service, and accommodating manager.
